# Standard library: reasoning
#
# The laws of logic a proof cites by name: excluded middle, double negation,
# and the rest of what takes a proof from some claims to another.
#
# One record per item. A record begins at column 0 with its kind, axiom,
# theorem or definition, and its name; `mundane` before the kind marks an
# item a proof takes for granted. A proof imports each item it cites,
# `import <header> stdlib/reasoning/<name>`, where the header is the
# record's own before its name, as `mundane theorem` or `axiom`. Format is described in DATABASE.md.
#
# An item's statement is written in the theorem form of SYNTAX.md: let and
# assume lines with labels, then a `then` line.
#
# Every item carries at least one of:
#   metamath    a set.mm label or labels supply it
#   open        it is cited but unproved and unbridged
#
# Other fields: target, note.


mundane axiom excluded-middle
  then        P or not P
  metamath    exmid
  target      exmid
  note        The first citation to substitute a formula for a variable rather
              than a term. A school reader has never been told that "P or not P"
              is a law, which is why it is cited and not folded into a method.


mundane theorem double-negation
  assume not not P                                                    (H1)
  then        P
  metamath    notnotr
  target      notnotr
  note        The step intuitionistic logic refuses, so citing it is the text
              saying which logic it is in. No proof cites it: a contradiction
              block proving a negation supposes what the negation denies, so
              none writes a doubled one. It stays for a line that meets one in
              what it cites, and tests/stdlib/reasoning.proof uses it.

mundane theorem disjunctive-syllogism
  assume P or Q                                                       (H1)
  assume not Q                                                        (H2)
  then        P
  metamath    orel2
  target      orel2
  note        The √2 pilot asked whether a school reader can check this on
              paper, and called it the acceptance test. Citing it puts the
              question where a reader can see it rather than inside a method.

mundane theorem or-left
  assume P                                                            (H1)
  then        P or Q
  metamath    orc
  target      orc
  note        A claim that one of two things holds, from a line saying the
              first does. Euclid's algorithm proves "b(k) = 0 or b(k) + k ≤
              N" at each k, knowing one side or the other.

mundane theorem or-right
  assume Q                                                            (H1)
  then        P or Q
  metamath    olc
  target      olc
  note        The same, from a line saying the second holds.
